    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Two different deformation mechanisms accommodate faulting in Jurassic sandstone units along the Moab normal fault in Utah, USA. These mechanisms are (1) the formation of deformation bands resulting from cataclastic shear failure of porous sandstone, and (2) the formation and subsequent shearing of joints producing splay fractures and breccia. We observe that formation of deformation bands consistently precedes jointing along the fault and is prevalent along the entire length of the fault system. In contrast, the formation and shearing of joints only contribute to fault development at geometrically complex portions of the fault trace such as intersections and extensional steps between fault segments. Contractional fault steps produce increased deformation band density within the step. The transition from deformation banding to jointing implies that fault and damage zone development may change during the slip history of a fault. Furthermore, this change first occurs at structurally complex locations as result of variations in the stress state around slipping faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
